.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAP-RESTART-APPLICATION 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eap-restart-application \- start an application's instances again
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eap restart-application
.BR \-\-application\-id | \-n
.I NAME
.SH DESCRIPTION
Records that this application's instances should be started again, and
returns immediately. The Euclid manager stops the whole pool on its next
reconcile, a few seconds later, and starts it straight back up from the
current definition \- the same thing it does after a redeploy, with
nothing new to pick up.
.PP
The desired state is never changed: as far as the manager is concerned
the application should be running throughout. So nothing is left stopped
if this command, or the connection carrying it, does not survive the
restart \- unlike stopping and starting it by hand, where an application
whose
.BR euclid-cli-eap-start-application (1)
never arrives stays down. An application that is already stopped is
refused rather than started.
.PP
Nothing about the application changes. The artifact, the environment and
the credentials all come back as they were, which is what makes this the
command for an instance that has to do its startup again \- a listener
that must subscribe afresh, a file read once at boot \- rather than a way
to deploy anything. Use
.BR euclid-cli-eap-redeploy-application (1)
for a new build.
.PP
The whole pool goes down and comes back together. That is the difference
from
.BR euclid-cli-emm-restart-module (1),
which cycles a module's instances one per reconcile tick so that the pool
keeps serving and no two instances are ever down at once. Applications
are modules to the manager, so that command works on one too; this is the
one to use when every instance has to have been through a restart before
anything else happens.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-application\-id " " \fINAME\fR
Name of the application. Required.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The restart was recorded. The application id and the number of instances
the manager is about to cycle are printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
Missing/invalid arguments, the caller is not an authenticated
administrator, no application of that name exists, or the application is
stopped.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Restart an application, then watch the pool come back:
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eap restart-application --application-id inbox
euclid-cli eap get-application --application-id inbox
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eap (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-start-application (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-stop-application (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-redeploy-application (1),
.BR euclid-cli-emm-restart-module (1)
